Qt Wayland: a resized window reuses its shm pools instead of a new pool per size (v3, set q4)

QWaylandShmBackingStore made a new wl_shm pool (memfd, which the kernel zero-fills before the client
paints over it) for every size of a window being resized, and deleted the others. This gives a free
buffer of another size the new size on its pool: the smallest pool that holds it without wasting
more than half of it, else the largest smaller one, grown with an eighth to spare (ftruncate, mremap,
wl_shm_pool.resize - pools only grow). Buffers of other sizes are kept while the size changes (they
take the next sizes) unless their pools are more than twice too large; those are pruned as before:
busy or free when no buffer could be reused, once free when one of the window's size is.

v1 (set q2, 2026-10-09) always took the largest free pool and never pruned until a buffer aged out:
a window made small kept the pools of its largest size (2026-10-14 qtm1, Konsole on a 4K screen at
200 %: 84 MiB resident after shrinking to 640x400, against 7.3 MiB with stock Qt). v2 (set q3)
bounded it but also pruned every buffer of another size whenever one of the window's size was free:
a window shows each size twice (a second commit with the finished content), so v2 lost its spare
pools at every size - bare metal 2026-10-14 qtb3, sizes shown per second over a 15 s oscillating
resize: Konsole stock 86 / v2 106-107 / v1 112-115, Dolphin 63-66 / 78-83 / 100-103.

QT_WAYLAND_SHM_NO_REUSE=1 turns it off (since v2 exactly the old pruning).

A pool grows by mremap where there is one (Linux), else by a new mapping. Each buffer keeps a
duplicate of its pool's descriptor (to grow the pool): up to MAX_BUFFERS (5) more open descriptors
per window. The class gains members: everything built against qwaylandshmbackingstore_p.h must be
rebuilt with it (the xdg-shell plugin constructs QWaylandShmBuffer for window icons - only
libQt6WaylandClient swapped in made Konsole and Dolphin abort on heap corruption, 2026-10-14 qtb1).

